ace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 3\\end{array}} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 14\\end{array}} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{blue}\\begin{array}{c} \\\\ 18\\end{array}} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{4} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{5} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{8} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} - \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 7 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 9 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  &  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 7 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 9 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike earns $\\$17,900$ more per year."] [17592186188166 :question/worked-solution "First, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones*, *tens* over *tens*, and *hundreds* over *hundreds*):\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the ones,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} - {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{blue}2}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ - & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 6; 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Subtracting the tens,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} - {\\color{blue}4} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{7}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{4}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{3}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Subtracting the hundreds,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} - {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cccc} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{5}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}-\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{2}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}4\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}6\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{3}}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n578 - 246 = 332\n$$"] [17592186188551 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r = \\sin^{2} θ$ for $θ ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})$. Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{′}(θ) = 2\\sin θ\\cos^{2} θ - \\sin^{3} θ, y^{′}(θ) = 3\\sin^{2} θ\\cos θ,\n$$\nand $y^{′}(θ) = 0$ at $θ = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x^{′}(0) = 0$\n2. $\\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$\n3. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $θ = 0$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188566 :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-2\\cdot (-1)}{0+2\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/2c/2c1b42bf3a8c1b5f8755e02a162e1554904bbc432285cb8ba8c7b06181e1cc03.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186188567 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1$ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0$\n\nII. $\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188567 :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,\\lim_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/51/515b4cbcb39ad80d4401adffd450aed9e222663c469f1cf1cf36cbd900b5eb4b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186188613 :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r = θ^{2}\\cos θ$ for $θ ∈ (- π, π)$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{′}(θ) = 2θ\\cos^{2} (θ) - θ^{2}\\sin (2θ), y^{′}(θ) = θ\\sin (2θ) + θ^{2}\\cos (2θ),\n$$\nand $x^{′}(0) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{′}(0) = 0$\n2. $\\lim_{θ \\to  0^{-}}\\frac{dy}{dx} = ∞$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $θ = 0$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188634 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{X | Y} ( x \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 3 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} , & x = 12 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} , & x = 18 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}Y=3]$ given that the conditional probability mass function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 3)$ is shown above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $μ_{X \\mid 3} = 16$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188655 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{X | Y} ( x \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{4}} , & \\hspace{1em} 1 \\le  x \\le  3 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[X\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}Y=2]$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid 2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $μ_{X \\mid 2} = \\frac{13}{6}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188719 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle f ( x , y ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{24 y}{7 ( 1 + x )}} , & \\displaystyle 0 \\le  x \\le  1 , \\hspace{0.278em} {x}^{2} \\le  y \\le  1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 , & \\displaystyle \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{1em} {f}_{X} ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{12 ( 1 - {x}^{4} )}{7 ( 1 + x )}} , & \\displaystyle 0 \\le  x \\le  1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 0 , & \\displaystyle \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}X=0]$ given that the corresponding expected value is $μ_{Y \\mid 0} = \\frac{2}{3}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188721 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{Y | X} ( y \\hspace{0.167em} | \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}} {y}^{2} , & \\hspace{1em} - 1 \\le  y \\le  1 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables.\nFind the conditional variance $\\text{Var}[Y\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}X=2]$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid 2)$ is shown above and the corresponding expected value is $μ_{Y \\mid 2} = 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186189374 :tutorial/content "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines' slopes grow to infinity as we approach $\\theta = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta-\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=\\cos\\theta - \\sin2\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\n- Applying L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the left-sided limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the limit in the denominator, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^-} \\left(\\cos\\theta - 2\\sin(2\\theta)\\right) = 0^-,\n$$\n\nwhere the notation $0^-$ means that the denominator approaches zero from the negative numbers.  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Using similar methods, it can be shown that the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, we conclude that there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/ea/ea0a03e575d962bd8d69505e20166dcc60c77cfe933f30762bb81296ccf7a797.png)"] [17592186189381 :tutorial/content "The first property to consider is how to recover the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y$ from the joint CDF. \n\nSuppose the joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}( 1 - {e}^{- x} ) ( 1 - {e}^{- 2 y} ) , & x \\ge  0 , \\hspace{0.278em} y \\ge  0 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise} .\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $F_Y(y),$ the marginal CDF of $Y$ using the following property:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y) = F(\\infty,y) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} F(x,y)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $y \\geq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[(1-{e}^{-x})(1-{e}^{-2y})] \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-2y})\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-2y})\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(1-0) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for the marginal CDF is as follows:\n\n$$\nF_Y(y)=\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}1 - {e}^{- 2 y} , & \\hspace{1em} y \\ge  0 \\\\ 0 , & \\hspace{1em} \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186189391 :tutorial/content "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach $\\theta = \\pi.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\pi$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\left(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (0)+0}{2\\cdot 1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)"] [17592186189446 :tutorial/content "Adding large numbers without using a calculator takes some effort. However, in some circumstances, it is sufficient to find an **estimate** for a particular sum. Computing an estimate is usually faster and easier than solving the original problem.\n\nTo estimate the answer to an addition problem, we round the numbers *first* and *then* add them.\n\nTo illustrate this, let's find an estimate for the value of\n\n$$\n382+567\n$$\n\nLet's start by rounding both numbers to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccc}382 & + & 567 \\\\ ↓ &  & ↓ \\\\ {\\color{blue}{380}} & + & {\\color{blue}{570}}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the sum ${\\color{blue}{380}} + {\\color{blue}{570}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ccccc} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\color{blue}{1\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}}} &  &  \\\\  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}3\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}8\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}+\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}7\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline  &  & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}9\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}5\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em} & \\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}0\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n382+567 \\approx 950\n$$\n\nThe symbol\n\n$$\n\\approx\n$$\n\nmeans \"is approximately equal to.\"\n\nThe estimate we've obtained is pretty close to the actual value of the sum. In fact,\n\n$$\n382+567=949\n$$\n\nIn this example, we rounded to the nearest ten. In the next example, we'll estimate a sum by rounding both numbers to the nearest hundred."]}
